# Ordered by importance/difficulty (quick high-value wins first, big/risky
# rewrites last). Tags: [S/M/L] = difficulty, [!!/!/.] = importance.

## Tier 1 - quick wins

[S/M][!] Browse... folder selection - DONE (pointing the dialog at the
    parent folder fixed it; confirmed working).

## Tier 2 - solid value, moderate effort

[M][!] script editor, group 1: everyday editing comfort - DONE (code_edit.rs,
    app/editor.rs)
    - line numbers, the error marked in the gutter and its message at
      its line (alignment was a problem once before: iterate on it with
      screenshots)
    - auto-indent: Enter keeps indentation, indents after function/then/
      do/{, `end` dedents itself
    - Tab / Shift+Tab indent a selection; Ctrl+/ toggles -- comments
    - auto-close brackets and quotes; highlight the matching bracket
    - find / replace (Ctrl+F, Ctrl+H), all matches highlighted
    - when edits apply: every edit (today), after a pause in typing, or
      only on Ctrl+S (every keystroke restarts the script now)
    - local history: last ~50 versions per script, History... to restore
    - zoom (Ctrl+scroll), word wrap toggle

[L][!] script editor, group 2: understanding Lua - DONE (lua_analysis.rs on
    full_moon 2.2; StyLua for formatting)
    - autocomplete popup as you type: host API, Lua stdlib (math.,
      string., table.), the script's locals and functions, fields of known
      tables (playback(). -> position, song_id, ...); Tab/Enter accepts
    - signature help inside a call, current parameter highlighted
    - warnings without running: undefined/misspelled globals, unused
      locals, accidental globals
    - outline (the script's functions), go to definition (Ctrl+click),
      hover for the script's own names
    - format document (StyLua)

[M][.] script editor, group 3: visual tools
    - color swatches next to {r, g, b} tables, click for a picker that
      rewrites the numbers - DONE (room left by the layouter, code_edit
      color_literals/set_color)
    - sprite editor: open a sprite_register call in a palette + pixel
      grid editor that writes image/palette back into the code - DONE
      (sprite_code.rs, app/sprite_editor.rs; sheet comment `-- sprite
      sheet: N frames of WxH`; New sprite...; Sprite editing layout).
      Ideas: rectangle/line tools, selection and move, flip, frame
      reorder, onion skin.
    - snippets: settings block, input_register controls, sprite
      template, typing_begin text field - DONE (snippets.rs, Insert menu;
      Insert > Sprite... uses the Sprite Editor's New sprite dialog)
    - hover help links into the Scripting Reference at that function -
      DONE (lua_docs::find; Ctrl+click too). editor_test.lua (undropped)
      exercises all the editor features.

[M][!] layout presets - DONE (layout::PRESETS: Listening, Watching, Script
    writing; saved layouts; each keeps changes made while active, built-ins
    have Reset). Add a Sprite editing preset with the sprite editor.
    Named layouts to switch between: media playback (songs, playlists,
    visualizer), script writing (editor, reference, settings, visualizer),
    sprite editing (sprite editor, editor, visualizer), etc. Built-ins
    plus saving your own; View > Layout.

[M][.] rename scripts - DONE
    Rename button by the script picker opens a modal; renames the .lua and
    its .settings.json together, refuses names Windows can't use or that
    are taken (case-only changes allowed), and repoints the running
    script, the picker and the editor at the new path. Works on bundled
    scripts too (they just stop offering Restore default).

[S][.] fuzzy search should index subdirectories as well - DONE (it
    already walked subfolders; confirmed working).

## Tier 3 - bigger undertakings

[L][!] playlists - v1 DONE, follow-ups below
    Saved, named, ordered lists (one JSON file each in
    <config dir>/playlists/), in a Playlists section. New > Blank, or New >
    From current folder (songs directly in the folder the Songs browser is
    showing). Drag songs from Songs into the list (between rows to insert,
    anywhere else in the panel to append; with no playlist yet, one gets
    made). Drag rows to reorder. Drag a soundfont from Soundfonts onto a
    MIDI row to give it an override ("SF: name" chip on the row; click it to
    clear). Right-click a row: play, use selected soundfont, clear override,
    show in Songs, remove. Rename / delete (click twice to confirm).
    Files dragged in from Explorer: songs (or whole folders) go into the
    shown playlist, .sf2 files join the Soundfonts list.
    Bottom bar: Previous (restarts if >3s in), Next, Shuffle (each entry
    once per pass, never the same track twice in a row across passes), and
    Loop cycles Off -> All (wrap the playlist) -> One (repeat this track,
    gaplessly in the engine). N / P keys for next/previous.
    Overrides swap back to the selected soundfont for entries without one;
    the last 3 parsed soundfonts stay cached so switching is instant after
    the first time.

    Follow-ups, not built yet:
    - DONE: songs and soundfonts load off the UI thread (loader.rs, two
      worker threads, path-keyed store). A clicked song shows
      "Loading..." and plays when ready; a MIDI file waits for its
      soundfont so it never starts on the wrong one. The next playlist
      track (decided as soon as the current one starts, even shuffled)
      and its soundfont preload in the background, as does the selected
      soundfont. Files nothing has needed for N seconds (Preferences,
      default 30) are unloaded, and at most 4 unneeded ones are kept
      regardless. Experimental (Preferences > Show experimental settings):
      preload a song after the pointer rests on it ~0.3 s in Songs or a
      playlist, plus a list of what's loaded right now.
      Preferences being open pauses playback (resumed on close if it was
      playing) and freezes the visualizer script. Script DT is now capped
      at 0.25 s so that pause, or a hidden Visualizer tab, doesn't jump
      animations.
      Not done: a load already running can't be cancelled, only dropped
      when it finishes; adding a soundfont via the dialog still parses it
      on the UI thread once (to validate it), then keeps that parse.
    - DONE: loop/shuffle mode remembered between launches (config).
    - DONE: multi-select (click selects, Ctrl/Shift extend, double-click
      plays, Ctrl+A, Delete; drag moves the selection; right-click and
      soundfont drops apply to it), folders dragged from Songs add their
      songs, .m3u/.m3u8 import and export (soundfont overrides kept as a
      #SYNTHTHING-SOUNDFONT: comment line).
    - DONE: song info (song_info.rs, background thread + cache): length in
      the Songs browser and playlist rows, playlist total, and an info
      button (greyed out when there's nothing) opening a modal of
      details: MIDI title/tracks/notes/channels/tempo/signature from
      midi_notes.rs, audio codec/rate/channels/tags via symphonia (no
      decoding). MP3s without a length header show --:--.
    - Needs your hands-on check: drag-and-drop feel (drop line placement,
      the label that follows the cursor), and that auto-advance fires
      reliably with the window in the background.

[M][!] panel layout rework - DONE (egui_dock)
    Sections (Songs, Soundfonts, Playlists, Visualizer, Script Editor,
    Script Settings, Scripting Reference) are dockable tabs: drag them next
    to each other, split any side, float them out into their own windows,
    resize. Closing a tab only hides it; View menu (top menu bar) brings it
    back. First launch opens Songs | Playlists only. The first time the
    editor is opened each run, the reference joins it as a tab in the same
    group (editor stays in front); Settings reappears below the visualizer.
    The arrangement is saved to the config (on show/hide, about once a
    second after any drag, and on exit) and restored at launch.
    A re-shown section goes back near its usual spot without disturbing
    the rest of a custom layout (layout.rs): next to its partner if that's
    open (Songs above Soundfonts, Visualizer left of the Editor), else the
    left/right edge for side sections, or carved out of the widest side
    leaf for the middle ones. Fullscreen is the same dock (the old
    visualizer-only fullscreen is gone). File > Preferences has "Separate
    layout for fullscreen" (off by default): off, fullscreen shows and
    edits the windowed layout; on, fullscreen keeps its own (starting as
    just the visualizer), saved separately and left untouched when the
    option is turned off again.
    Playback controls stay pinned at the bottom, outside the dock.
    Needs your hands-on check: where re-shown tabs land, and that dragging
    tabs and dragging songs/soundfonts into playlists don't trip each
    other up.

[L][.] auto updating system - DONE (GitHub Releases, Dissssy/synththing)
    Releasing: `cargo release patch` (dry run), then
    `cargo release patch --execute` bumps Cargo.toml, commits, tags vX.Y.Z
    and pushes; the tag runs .github/workflows/release.yml (checks the tag
    matches Cargo.toml, tests, builds release, publishes the GitHub Release
    with synththing-windows-x86_64.exe and generated notes).
    App (src/updater.rs): release builds check GitHub at startup
    (Preferences toggle) and open an Updates window only if there's a newer
    version that wasn't skipped; Help > Check for updates any time.
    Install downloads in the background, checks size and GitHub's SHA-256
    for the asset, swaps the running exe (self-replace), then Restart now /
    Later. Debug builds never check on launch and can't install.
    Not done: code signing (SmartScreen will warn testers on first run of
    an unsigned exe), release notes beyond GitHub's generated ones, a
    project README/license.

## Additional/unsorted

[M][.] mouse/keyboard input for lua scripts - DONE
    mouse(), mouse_delta(), scroll(), mouse_down/pressed/released([button]),
    key_down/pressed/released(name), has_focus(), display_mode() ("window",
    "fullscreen", "dedicated"), set_cursor_visible(), set_cursor_locked().
    Mouse while the pointer is over the visualizer; keys only while it has
    focus (click in, click elsewhere to leave), and the app's own shortcuts
    are off meanwhile. Escape is never reported to scripts: it releases
    focus and exits the dedicated fullscreen.
    "Fullscreen visualizer" button above the visualizer: OS fullscreen with
    only the visualizer, focused, a 3 s "Press Esc to exit" hint; Esc goes
    back to the layout (and out of OS fullscreen unless it was already).
    Hidden cursor only applies while over the visualizer; cursor locking is
    a confine-to-window (Windows can't do a true relative-motion lock) and
    only takes effect in the dedicated fullscreen, with focus, while the
    window is focused, so a script can never trap the cursor.
    input_demo.lua (undropped, New > input_demo): paint toy using all of it.
    App-wide keys, work from anywhere and never reach scripts: Esc (from
    either fullscreen straight back to windowed), F11 (toggle the dedicated
    fullscreen, back to wherever you were), F5 (restart the running script:
    fresh VM, same source and settings; also a Restart button by the script
    picker).
    Keyboard input is now actions (input_register/input/input_down,
    rebindable under Script Settings > Controls, saved to
    <script>.lua.controls.json) plus text_typed() and typing spans
    (typing_begin/typing_state/typing_end, editing in src/typing.rs;
    actions are blocked while a span is active). key_down/key_pressed/
    key_released still work but are out of the docs. Examples: snake and
    input_demo use actions, terminal.lua (undropped) is a typing span.
    Ideas not done: gamepad bindings for actions (the Controls list is
    where they'd go), mouse buttons as action bindings.

[M][.] scripts play notes - DONE
    play_note / note_on / note_off / stop_notes / notes_playable and
    sequences (sequence_register in beats, sequence_play at the song's
    tempo, sequence_stop). A second synthesizer (src/live.rs) copies the
    song synth's channel state every block (vendored LOCAL PATCH:
    Synthesizer::copy_channels_from) and renders into its own ~25 ms ring,
    mixed by rodio, so notes are heard quickly instead of after the song's
    buffer. Only while a MIDI with a soundfont is loaded; channel falls
    back to the song's lowest. Stopped on recompile/restart, song load,
    and when the visualizer stops being drawn.
    keyboard.lua plays along: A-L a major-scale span, arrows move it and
    change the key.
    Script notes are in active_notes() with source = "script".
    Their audio is in the visualizer tap too (SampleTap::push_live: mixed
    into the song's samples one for one while it plays, alone while it
    doesn't), so waveform/fft/levels/onset react to them.
    Ideas not done: a hardware MIDI input (parked: nothing to test with); other scales;
    sequence looping.

[S][.] a real doc system for the lua scripting API - DONE
    One page, the Scripting Reference tab. The source is
    docs/scripting-reference.md (plain Markdown, readable on GitHub and easy
    to hand to an AI); build.rs parses a small subset of it (## sections,
    paragraphs, fenced code, - bullets, inline `code`, <!-- comments -->)
    into lua_docs.rs's Section/Block table at build time, and a malformed
    file (unterminated fence, text before the first ##) fails the build.
    The in-app hover help (lua_completion::HOST_API) is still separate,
    one line per function.

[S][.] lua language server / vscode-like script editor - DONE for the scoped
    v1, rest intentionally parked below
    Full LSP (external lua-language-server process, JSON-RPC client) turned
    out to be the wrong tool anyway, a generic Lua LSP has no idea about our
    custom globals (clear, setting_int, playback, ...) without custom
    annotations it doesn't have. What shipped instead:
    - Syntax highlighting (lua_highlight.rs) via TextEdit::layouter, a
      hand-rolled tokenizer (keywords/strings/comments/numbers, plus our own
      host functions colored distinctly), no tokenizer crate needed, Lua's
      lexical grammar is small.
    - A small "internal LSP" (lua_completion.rs): a background thread per
      script-change request, discarding a result if a newer request has
      since been made (generation counter, not real cancellation, today's
      analysis is instant anyway, but the shape is there for when it isn't).
      Feeds a "Functions" dropdown in the editor that inserts the picked
      name at the text cursor (via TextEditState's documented cursor
      load/store API).
    - Hover info: mouse-hovering a known identifier, or pressing F1 at the
      text cursor, looks it up against the same host-API table and shows
      its one-line doc above the editor. Cursor->word mapping goes through
      the galley (Galley::cursor_from_pos) for the mouse case and
      TextEditOutput::cursor_range for the F1 case.

    Deliberately NOT built (parked per your "park that for now" call):
    - Real source parsing: the completion/hover analysis is still just the
      static HOST_API table, blind to the actual script. `analyze()` in
      lua_completion.rs already takes the source string for exactly this;
      next step would be finding locals in scope at the cursor and the
      variable's inferred kind (e.g. "this came from setting_color, so it's
      a color").
    - Lua stdlib completion/hover: math.*/string.*/table.* members aren't in
      HOST_API at all right now, only our own host functions.
    - DONE: error-line highlighting. The line an error points at (parsed
      from mlua's [string "visualizer"]:N: prefix) is underlined in red,
      and "Go to line N" by the error (editor, or visualizer, which opens
      the editor) moves the text cursor there and scrolls to it.

[S][.] debug variable inspector in the Settings popup - DONE
    Not a `lua.globals()` dump, every bundled script correctly uses `local`
    for its state, which globals() can't see at all (locals aren't a Lua
    *value* reachable through any table, just stack slots in a running
    frame), so that would've shown almost nothing. Went with your
    alternative instead: a `debug_locals([label])` host function a script
    calls at whatever specific point it wants a snapshot from. Reads real
    locals via the debug *C* API (lua_getstack + lua_getlocal on the calling
    frame, through mlua's exec_raw_lua escape hatch), a small, bounded
    unsafe block, verified by a real test (not just "it compiled"). Does NOT
    grant scripts Lua's own `debug` library; StdLib::DEBUG stays excluded
    from the sandbox, this is the host using the C API on itself. Shows as a
    nested CollapsingHeader tree in Settings, between the settings grid and
    the log, capped in depth/breadth so a huge table (snake.lua's `occ`,
    say) can't make one call expensive. Only sees true locals/parameters,
    not module-level `local`s outside the calling function (those are
    upvalues, a different thing), called from inside render(), it won't
    see e.g. waveform.lua's `history`, only whatever render() itself
    declares or receives as a parameter.

    A clickable line-number gutter (click a line to mark it, injecting
    `debug_locals()` there automatically instead of typing it) was tried and
    reverted, a button per source line added real lag, and lining a
    hand-rolled gutter up pixel-for-pixel with TextEdit's own line layout
    wasn't worth what it bought. Settled on documenting the function
    properly instead: it's now in lua_completion::HOST_API (autocomplete,
    hover info, syntax highlighting, the works) and has its own section on
    the Docs page. Calling it by hand is the whole interface.

[S][!] central app log - DONE
    No console in release builds, so applog.rs is the `log` crate's logger:
    in memory for Help > Log... (a modal: level filter, search, copy, open
    log folder, clear) and written to <config dir>/synththing.log, with the
    previous run's kept as synththing.prev.log. App messages from Info up,
    other crates (egui/wgpu/winit) from Warn up. Logged: every status-line
    message, failed song/soundfont loads, update checks and installs, and
    panics on any thread (with a backtrace) via a panic hook.
    Lua scripts' own log()/errors stay in each script's log (Script
    Settings tab), not here.

[S][.] CI on every push - DONE
    .github/workflows/ci.yml builds and runs the tests on every push to
    master and every pull request (release.yml still does releases).

[S][.] live refresh of folders on screen - DONE
    watch.rs (notify crate, OS change notifications, no polling) watches
    the Songs browser's current folder and the scripts folder; changes
    settle for 300 ms, then the browser re-lists (search results too) and
    the script picker refreshes. If the running script's own file changed
    (edited in another editor), it's reloaded into the editor and
    visualizer; the app's own saves match the editor, so they don't.
    Not watched: saved playlists (their missing-file marks update when a
    track fails to load) and the soundfont list (it's a list, not a folder).

## Script API wishlist (from a script author's feedback, 2026-10-02)
# In the author's order of how much each would have helped. Tags as above.

[S][!!] document units (item 2) - DONE, in docs/scripting-reference.md.
    The answers, for reference:
    - playback().position / length: seconds of song time.
    - upcoming_notes() seconds_until and NOTE_LOOKAHEAD (4.0): song time
      too, not wall-clock. Speed changes how fast song time passes (at 2x,
      one song-second takes 0.5 s), so wall-clock = seconds_until / speed.
    - colors: r/g/b 0-255 (clamped, fractions allowed), a 0.0-1.0.
    - fft_left/right: 512 magnitudes (FFT_SIZE 1024 / 2), bin i is
      i * SAMPLE_RATE / 1024 Hz (~43.07 Hz apart at 44.1 kHz), from a ~23 ms
      Hann-windowed window; a full-scale sine reads ~1.0; empty table until
      the first 1024 samples have played.

[M][!!] notes_between(t0, t1) -> {channel, key, velocity, start, stop, id}
    - DONE without touching the vendored rustysynth: midi_notes.rs reads
      the Standard MIDI File itself on the loader thread (note on/off
      paired first-in first-out per channel+key, ticks to seconds through
      the tempo map, checked against rustysynth's own timing in a test).
      Any window, held notes included, id = stable index. The same reader
      can provide the tempo map / time signature for beat() later, still
      without vendor changes.
    Pairing note-offs to note-ons in Lua is the fiddliest part of game
    scripts (upcoming_notes repeats events every frame until they play, so
    scripts dedupe by float timestamps). Pair them once in Rust at MIDI
    load (the vendored MidiFile has every message and its time), keep the
    note list shared with the visualizer, answer any window (not limited
    to NOTE_LOOKAHEAD, so whole-song/level building works), give each note
    a stable id (its index), and include notes already held at t0 (fixes
    the after-seek / script-start gap). Could also add an id to
    upcoming_notes entries as the smaller step.

[M][!] text drawing - DONE (pixel_font.rs: Monogram, CC0, by Vinícius
    Menézio; text() returns width/height, text_size() measures, height is
    the line height in pixels, FONT_HEIGHT = 12). Original plan:
    Three scripts so far wrote their own bitmap font. Plan:
    - An existing pixel-art font, embedded in the binary, drawn with pure
      nearest-neighbor scaling (no smoothing), through the same command
      queue as rect/line.
    - The script gives a pixel height, not a font size, for easy layout:
      text(x, y, string, color, height) -> width drawn. Heights that are a
      whole multiple of the font's native height stay perfectly crisp;
      other heights still work (nearest-neighbor), just with uneven pixels.
    - Measuring without drawing, for laying out tables first:
      text_width(string, height), or a dry-run flag on text().
    - Candidate fonts, all free to embed (check the license file when
      picking): Monogram (CC0), Pixel Operator (CC0), Spleen (BSD), Cozette
      (MIT), Press Start 2P (OFL). Needs a pick.

[S][!] seek/loop generation in playback() - DONE (playback().generation)
    A counter that bumps on every seek, loop and new song, so scripts
    detect discontinuities exactly instead of guessing from position
    jumps. Engine-side counter, passed through EngineView.

[S][!] persistent script storage: store_get(key) / store_set(key, value) - DONE
    (<script>.lua.store.json; tables supported; Rename moves it too)
    Script-owned saved state (high scores, unlocks), separate from the
    user-facing settings. A <script>.lua.store.json sidecar next to the
    settings one; bool/number/string values (maybe nested tables via
    JSON); written when changed, debounced. Rename should carry it along
    like the settings sidecar.

[S][!] playback control from scripts: set_paused(bool), seek(t) - DONE
    (allowed always, like set_channel_enabled)
    Pause on death, "retry this section", practice loops. Same pattern as
    set_channel_enabled (queued requests, the app sends AudioCommands).
    Worth deciding whether it's allowed always or only while focused.

[M][.] musical timing - DONE: beat(), time_at_beat(), bar(), tempo(),
    time_signature(), from midi_notes.rs's tempo map (no vendor changes).
    Original note: beat() (position in beats), tempo, time signature
    For beat-synced effects and rhythm-game judgment windows. Now doable
    without patching the vendored rustysynth: midi_notes.rs already reads
    tempo events (FF 51); add time signatures (FF 58) and keep both as a
    map, then beat() is a lookup. Nothing for plain audio files.

[S/M][.] cheap audio features - DONE: level_left/right() (RMS), onset()
    (spectral flux, adaptive threshold, 80 ms refractory; only runs once a
    script asks). Original: level_left/right() (RMS), onset flag
    RMS is trivial in Rust over the frame's samples. Onset (e.g. spectral
    flux over the FFT the analyzer already does, with a threshold) is a
    bit more, but saves every audio-reactive script reinventing it.

[S/M][.] more drawing primitives - DONE: circle(), triangle(), polygon()
    (pixel-center coverage, even-odd fill, blended once). Original: filled
    circle, triangle/polygon
    Scanline fills in rasterize(), alpha-blended like rect.
[M][.] sprites - DONE (2026-10-02 design): scripts register palette-indexed
    images as Lua data, sprite_register({image = rows, palette = colors}),
    converted to packed colors once; sprite(id, x, y, scale/options) draws
    nearest-neighbor with flips. No image files involved. Could add later:
    rotation, drawing a sub-rectangle (sprite sheets), loading .png from
    the scripts folder.
    Needs an image decoder (png crate or similar), a per-script texture
    cache, and a blit command with scaling. Bigger, but natural if games
    keep being the main use.

Smaller:
[S][.] FRAME counter and an unclamped wall clock (TIME) - DONE
[M][!] headless script runner - DONE as `synththing run-script` (headless.rs,
    cli.rs with clap; release exe re-attaches to the launching terminal via
    AttachConsole when given arguments; documented in docs/cli.md; fixed
    1/fps timestep). Original notes:
    synththing --run-script file.lua
    --frames N [--midi song.mid], feeding a MIDI file through the engine
    without a window and reporting runtime errors, so script authors can
    test without mocking the API.
    Console question: windows_subsystem is decided at compile time, per
    exe, so one exe can't be a GUI app when double-clicked and a console
    app when run from a terminal. Options:
    - A second small binary (src/bin, e.g. synththing-cli.exe) built as a
      console app, sharing the engine and script code, shipped alongside.
      Cleanest: the terminal waits for it and gets its output and exit
      code. (Recommended.)
    - Keep one exe and AttachConsole(ATTACH_PARENT_PROCESS) when run with
      CLI flags: output shows in the terminal, but the shell doesn't wait
      for it, so prompts and output interleave and exit codes are lost.
    - Make the exe a console app and hide the console when it's a GUI
      launch: a console window flashes up on double-click.

[S][.] credits & licenses modal - DONE
    Help > Credits & licenses: GitHub contributors (fetched on first
    open), Monogram attribution + its credits.txt, Lua's license, and all
    libraries in the Windows build with license texts
    (assets/licenses/third_party.json from `cargo run --example
    gen_licenses`; a test fails if it drifts from Cargo.lock).
    Release checklist (incl. keeping docs/scripting-reference.md and
    docs/cli.md current before every commit): docs/RELEASING.md.

[S][.] README for testers - DONE (README.md: what it is, download, the
    SmartScreen "Run anyway" step, first steps, keys, scripts, where files
    live, building, credits). Still open: a license for synththing itself
    (the repo has none, so it's all rights reserved by default).

[S][.] friend feedback (game visualizer) - DONE
    sprite() palette = {[i] = color} and tint = color, per draw (sprites
    keep palette indices next to their baked colors). playback() has
    song_name, song_path and song_id (16 hex digits of the file's
    SHA-256, loader::song_id) for per-song saved data.

[M][!] visuals in step with the sound - DONE
    The ring buffer runs buffer_ms ahead of the speakers. AudioRing keeps a
    lock-free Backlog (queued + consumer-held samples); SampleTap hands the
    visualizer only audio that's played (song and live synth each held
    back by their own backlog); the render thread records a Moment
    (position, generation, paused, finished, score notes) per block and
    publishes the one being heard. Script notes stay current. Also fixed:
    a flush is done by the render thread under the ring lock, so audio
    rendered right after a seek is no longer thrown away by the consumer.
    Not counted: the OS/device's own output latency.

[L][!] recording the visualizer to video - DONE
    recorder.rs: raw frames piped to ffmpeg (libx264 veryfast crf 18,
    temp .mkv) on a writer thread, audio to a temp float WAV, muxed to
    .mp4 (AAC) on Stop. Video frames are counted off the audio, so they
    can't drift; silence keeps time while nothing plays; repeats instead
    of stalling when the encoder lags (live), waits for it (run-script);
    frames handed over by swapping buffers. While recording, the script
    renders exactly when a video frame is due, with DT = the video time
    passed (even motion). Quality: standard (4:2:0, tune animation, crf
    16, BT.709 tagged), sharp (4:4:4), lossless (RGB); warning past 1080p
    and "frames repeating" on the REC badge. The panel renders at the recording size (letterboxed on
    screen, mouse mapped). ffmpeg.rs: the downloaded copy in the config
    folder or one on the PATH; on Windows, offers gyan.dev's release
    essentials (SHA-256 checked, only ffmpeg.exe kept). Record menu: this
    song from the start (stops at its end or when it starts over), this
    playlist from the start (stops after the last track), or free; F9/F10.
    Preferences > Recording: size (720p-4K), 30/60 fps, folder
    (Videos\synththing). run-script --video renders offline with the
    same recorder. Ideas: render a song to video from the app (offline,
    frame-perfect, any speed), scripts knowing they're being recorded.

[S][!] scripts drive playback - DONE
    playlist() (read-only: the playing playlist, else the open one),
    play_track / next_track / previous_track, set_speed, set_loop,
    set_shuffle; playback() has loop_mode and shuffle. Only songs already
    in the playlist; volume stays the user's.
    script_options({ start_paused = true }): songs load paused while that
    script runs (for games with a start button); playback().song_loads
    counts loads, the same song again included.

[L][.] shared script library (long term)
    A place to share scripts (they're sandboxed: the worst a script can do
    is spam notes or mute channels). Maybe `synththing serve` as a web
    server (needs a Linux build) that renders each upload with a default
    song through run-script --video's path: a 5 s preview to play in the
    app and a thumbnail from the most striking frame of it.

[S][.] reference: Example calls and results - DONE
    Real return values (captured with a probe script through run-script)
    for every function returning a table or several values. Refresh them
    when a return shape changes.

[M][!] channel mutes without a skip - DONE
    Toggling a channel used to flush the audio buffer (buffer_ms of
    rendered-ahead audio thrown away), skipping the song and the visuals
    forward. Now the render thread keeps a synth snapshot per block still
    unheard (rustysynth LOCAL PATCH: Clone), and a toggle rewinds to the
    block being heard, applies it there and re-renders the buffered part:
    heard at once, nothing skipped (audio::tests::muting_a_channel_doesnt_skip
    checks it sample for sample against the real synth).

[S][!] bundled scripts by kind - DONE
    assets/visualizers/{visualizers,games,templates,examples}; build.rs
    generates BUNDLED_SCRIPTS (category, name, contents). Visualizers and
    games are added to the user's folder, each once (the .seeded marker
    lists them; an empty one means an 0.1.8 install); templates and
    examples only under New. Picker and New are grouped by kind.
    highway.lua (games) is new; game.lua is now note_runner.lua.

[S][.] scripts see a song switch all at once - DONE
    The app hands the script a new song's notes/id/path only when the
    audio thread reports it loaded (EngineView::loads), so playback() never
    mixes the old song's length with the new song's notes.

[M][!] game controllers - DONE
    gamepad.rs (gilrs): every connected pad merged; buttons, d-pad, triggers
    and stick directions as on/off inputs (past halfway, with hysteresis)
    that actions bind like keys ("pad_a", "pad_lstick_left", ...; captured
    in the Controls list too). pad_axis() reads sticks/triggers directly
    (not rebindable), gamepads() lists them. Pads count while the window has
    focus. Ideas: rumble, per-player pads (pad 1 vs pad 2) for local
    multiplayer, pad defaults in the bundled games.

[M][!] analogue values for actions
    input_value(id) -> 0..1 for any action: keys and buttons 0 or 1, a
    trigger how far it's pulled, a stick direction (pad_lstick_left) how far
    it's pushed that way. input()/input_down() keep the threshold, so
    nothing existing changes. Steering becomes input_value(RIGHT) -
    input_value(LEFT), the same from keys or a stick and fully rebindable;
    pad_axis stays as a convenience. Update the docs and gamepad_demo.lua.

[M][.] grouped controls and settings, shown as tabs
    Let scripts put controls (and settings) in named groups, e.g.
    input_register("jump", { "space", "pad_a" }, { group = "Movement" }) and
    a group option on setting_*; Script Settings shows each group as a
    selectable tab button above its list, ungrouped ones under "General".
    For scripts like gamepad_demo.lua with dozens of controls.
    Probably as its own modal opened from the Visualizer tab (a Controls
    button) rather than inside Script Settings, laid out like Preferences:
    groups down the side, one line per control, reusing its rendering
    (with_info, the fixed-height body) so the two look alike.

[S][.] very large MIDI warning - DONE
    Over 100,000 notes (song_info::HEAVY_MIDI_NOTES): a red ! by the length
    in the lists, and a "play anyway?" window before playing, with "don't
    warn me again" (and a Preferences checkbox). highway.lua takes ~3 s on
    one frame for 150k notes (debug build).
    Watchdog - DONE: an mlua hook every 10,000 instructions stops a run of
    script code (top level or one render()) past 3 s with an error, and
    render() isn't called again until the script changes or restarts.
    Script thread - DONE (script_host.rs): the LuaVisualizer lives on its
    own thread; the app sends messages and frame requests and reads back a
    status snapshot, waiting up to 50 ms for each frame (most are shown at
    once). A frame busy for over 1 s gets a notice in the visualizer's
    bottom-left with a Stop button (the watchdog's hook also checks a stop
    flag); anything replacing a busy script stops it first. The watchdog
    allows 10 s in the app, 3 s in run-script.
    Idea not done: highway charting a few tracks per frame.
